WEBVTT

00:00:00.095 --> 00:00:02.351
This is someone shopping with only a keyboard.

00:00:03.295 --> 00:00:07.242
They press Tab to move through the page… and the Add to cart button gets skipped.

00:00:07.317 --> 00:00:08.599
They can't buy anything.

00:00:08.795 --> 00:00:16.688
That's not rare. In 2026, 95.9 percent of the top million home pages had accessibility failures.

00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:18.206
So we built Curb Cut.

00:00:18.281 --> 00:00:23.145
It fixes barriers from the outside, right in your browser, and proves every fix works.

00:00:23.328 --> 00:00:27.894
Step one. Kane, an AI testing agent, tries the keyboard. It fails.

00:00:28.095 --> 00:00:30.655
Kane tries a mouse. The same task works.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.047
So the website is broken for keyboard users.

00:00:33.228 --> 00:00:38.090
Step two. An agent writes safe rules: make the button reachable, and let Enter press it.

00:00:38.295 --> 00:00:44.588
Step three. Kane reaches the button, presses Enter, and the headphones go in the cart. A recording is proof.

00:00:44.795 --> 00:00:48.552
Step four. The extension shares verified fixes with everyone.

00:00:48.762 --> 00:00:53.153
The hard barriers aren't single buttons. They're menus that only open for a mouse.

00:00:53.228 --> 00:00:58.369
On mass.edu, a keyboard user reaches About, and the menu never opens.

00:00:58.444 --> 00:01:00.237
The links are there, but invisible.

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:07.160
With Curb Cut, the same keys open the menu, every link is visible as you reach it, and Enter takes you to the page.

00:01:07.362 --> 00:01:12.012
Kane proved it both ways: without the fix it failed, with the fix it reached the page.

00:01:12.195 --> 00:01:17.848
On csc.gov.in, ABOUT blocked the keyboard too. Now it opens About MeitY.

00:01:18.028 --> 00:01:22.231
Screen reader users heard an unnamed box. Now it's called Sites search.

00:01:22.428 --> 00:01:25.330
At high zoom, text ran offscreen. Now it wraps.

00:01:25.628 --> 00:01:29.895
On ColorHexa, “I'm stuck” reports the picker. Kane verifies the fix.

00:01:30.095 --> 00:01:34.806
Then we locked the code and pointed it at 2,050 websites it had never seen.

00:01:34.881 --> 00:01:45.974
It found 142 real barriers and fixed 99 of them on its own, including 51 of the 89 hard ones: menus, expanding panels, tabs and pop-ups.

00:01:46.162 --> 00:01:52.007
On one of those new sites, the World Register of Marine Species, the keyboard skipped the Tools menu.

00:01:52.082 --> 00:01:55.133
Kane failed it without the fix, and passed it with the fix.

00:01:55.328 --> 00:02:00.531
Fixes ship only after Kane proves them. 43 are live in the extension today.

00:02:00.728 --> 00:02:09.058
Every night, Kane tries each task with the fix turned off. When the website fixes itself, the patch retires. It becomes unnecessary.

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:13.635
Curb Cut. Fixing the web for the people it shuts out. Verified by Kane.
